  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nMultiple embedded mid level vortices have developed along the\neastern periphery of developing upper level trough. Initial wave\nwas located over the SE Catskills will continue tracking\nnorthward through daybreak, with moderate to heavy rain on its\neastern side persisting across the NE Catskills into the upper\nHudson Valley region. Rainfall rates up to 1-2\"/hour will be\npossible, especially in favored upslope areas in the NE\nCatskills, and possibly NW Saratoga/western Warren Cos.\nAdditional showers/thunderstorms with heavy rain will continue\ntranslating N/NE into the Taconics, NW CT/western MA and\nsouthern VT toward daybreak, with some rainfall rates \npotentially reaching up to 2\"/hour.\n\nAs this initial wave rides north, another feature located off\nthe NJ coast should lift toward LI and then southern New England\nlater this morning and afternoon. This should allow an area of\nmoderate to heavy rain across western New England to pivot\nwestward, reaching areas near and north of I-90 including the\nCapital Region, the Lake George/Saratoga and upper Hudson Valley\nregions later this morning through mid afternoon. Rainfall rates\ncould still reach 1-2\"/hour within this area, which added to the\nprior rainfall, could increase chances for flash flooding,\nespecially in poor drainage and urban areas. Therefore, the\nFlood Watch for Flash Flooding has been expanded northward into\nthese areas.\n\nHeavy rain should decrease in intensity toward and after sunset,\nwith a general decrease in areal coverage of showers tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLatest HEFS (Ensemble River Forecasts) continue to show a low \n(generally <10-15%) chance for main stem river flooding with \nthis event, likely due to antecedent dry conditions and rainfall\ndeficits. However, rainfall amounts are siding on the higher end\nof forecasts, and latest NERFC forecasts do suggest the Hoosic\nRiver @ Williamstown could approach minor flood stage early this\nafternoon, with Esopus Creek @ Cold Brook also approaching minor\nFS early this morning. Will have to watch other points across\nthe eastern Catskills and western New England through today\ngiven that higher rainfall totals are most likely going to be \nrealized.",
